sections.text:
Mixing units is the most common error. Moments in kN·m and section properties in mm⁴ must be converted before use.

Other common errors are:
forgetting the self-weight of a member;
using an ultimate load where a service load is required, or the reverse;
using total stress where effective stress is required in soil problems;
confusing the Darcy friction factor with the smaller factor used in some older books;
copying a formula from a code without checking its conditions and the units it assumes;
treating software output as correct because it looks precise.

Also state your assumptions. An examiner, like a senior engineer, needs to see how you reached the answer as well as the answer itself.

An engineer’s first duty is to the safety, health and welfare of the public. Common ethical rules include:
work only within your competence;
be honest in reports, tests and certificates;
declare conflicts of interest;
refuse bribes and report corruption through the proper channels;
respect the environment and the people affected by a project;
keep your knowledge up to date.

For example, Amina, a site engineer, finds that test cubes from a pour have failed. The client wants to continue in order to meet a deadline. She should record the result, report it to the responsible engineer and insist on investigation before more load is added.

Negligent engineers can also be liable, and this law differs by country.

Good practice includes:
check that the reactions balance the applied loads;
refine the mesh until the key results stop changing, which is a convergence study;
compare with a hand calculation or a published benchmark;
investigate stress peaks at sharp corners and point supports, because they can grow without limit as the mesh is refined.

Common errors are wrong units, wrong supports and poor element shapes.
